        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"partial\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"direct\",\n  \"useful_material\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"Targeted LC-MS/MS parameters for inosine quantification in human serum: Kinetex HILIC column (150 × 3 mm, 2.6 μm), isocratic elution (0.2% formic acid in water:methanol 10:90), flow rate 0.25 mL/min, column temp 50 °C, negative ionization mode, MRM transitions monitored (specific transitions listed in Supplementary Table S1 of the study).\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Directly addresses success criterion (1) for analytical platform parameters for inosine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"Sample processing standards for serum metabolites: Blood collected in clotting activator tubes, sera aliquoted and stored at −80 °C, thawed on ice, protein precipitation using ice-cold methanol (350 µL to 100 µL serum), vortexed and centrifuged (13,000g, 10 min, 4 °C).\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Directly addresses success criterion (2) for sample processing standards (extraction, storage).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"Absolute concentration ranges for inosine in cancer patients: Lung Cancer (LC) group mean 5.88 μM (median 5.38 μM) in discovery set; mean 5.54 μM (median 3.72 μM) in validation set. COPD control group mean 2.36 μM (median 1.15 μM) in discovery set.\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Directly addresses success criterion (3) for reference concentration ranges in cancer patients, though controls are disease-matched (COPD) rather than healthy.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"Targeted LC-MS/MS parameters for succinic acid quantification: Same HILIC method as inosine, negative ionization mode, validated according to EMA guidelines with LOQ, precision (CV < 13.8%), and accuracy (%bias < 14.8%) reported.\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Provides protocol for succinic acid, a TCA cycle intermediate often analyzed alongside SCFAs, though chemically distinct from microbiome-derived SCFAs (acetate/propionate/butyrate).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"auxiliary_material\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"Method validation details: Calibration curves constructed using linear regression with surrogate matrix (2% bovine albumin in PBS), stability tests (autosampler, bench-top, freeze-thaw) indicating significant decrease (>15%) of inosine concentration after freeze-thaw or bench-top exposure.\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Highlights critical pre-analytical conditions affecting inosine stability, useful for protocol optimization.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"Confounding factor analysis: Study demonstrates that smoking status and medication (e.g., theophylline) significantly impact metabolite profiles, necessitating strict matching or stratification in biomarker studies.\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Contextual warning for interpreting reference ranges; suggests that 'standard' ranges may vary by host factors like smoking.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"Comparison of untargeted vs. targeted workflows: Untargeted LC-HRMS used for discovery, targeted triple-quadrupole MS used for absolute quantification, emphasizing the need for both steps in robust biomarker identification.\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Methodological context for how quantification protocols are typically derived in this field.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"remaining_gap\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"Standardized protocols for canonical short-chain fatty acids (acetate, propionate, butyrate) are not provided; the study quantifies succinic acid, which is a dicarboxylic acid and not typically classified as a microbiome-derived SCFA in the context of ICI research.\",\n      \"impact\": \"The gap question explicitly requests SCFA protocols; this remains unmet for the specific metabolites most relevant to the microbiome topic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"Reference ranges are provided against a COPD control group rather than healthy controls; while cancer patient ranges are available, healthy baseline ranges for inosine are not explicitly